REFERENCE   13 (residues 1 to 347)
  AUTHORS   Jost,A. and Pfeifer,F.
  TITLE     Interaction of the gas vesicle proteins GvpA, GvpC, GvpN, and GvpO
            of Halobacterium salinarum
  JOURNAL   Front Microbiol 13, 971917 (2022)
   PUBMED   35966690
  REMARK    SUBUNIT, AND SUBCELLULAR LOCATION.;
            STRAIN=PHH1; PLASMID=pHH1
            Publication Status: Online-Only
COMMENT     On or before Jun 21, 2005 this sequence version replaced
            gi:12644336, gi:7484029.
            [FUNCTION] An ATPase that functions in gas vesicle formation (By
            similarity). A minor component of the gas vesicle, also found in
            soluble extracts (PubMed:21158390). Probably enhances gas vesicle
            formation (Probable). Gas vesicles are hollow, gas filled
            proteinaceous nanostructures found in several microbial planktonic
            microorganisms. They allow positioning of halobacteria at the
            optimal depth for growth in the poorly aerated, shallow brine pools
            of their habitat (PubMed:33711860). {ECO:0000250|UniProtKB:Q8YUT0,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:21158390, ECO:0000269|PubMed:33711860,
            ECO:0000305|PubMed:8606186}.
            [FUNCTION] Expression of a 9.5 kb p-vac DNA fragment containing 2
            divergently transcribed regions
            (gvpD-gvpE-gvpF-gvpG-gvpH-gvpI-gvpJ-gvpK-gvpL-gvpM and
            gvpA-gvpC-gvpN-gvpO) allows H.volcanii to produce gas vesicles
            (PubMed:10894744, PubMed:1404376, PubMed:7651141). A similar region
            restores gas vesicle production in H.halobium without the p-vac
            locus, but which still have the c-vac locus (PubMed:1398080,
            PubMed:8002589, PubMed:8423144). {ECO:0000269|PubMed:10894744,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:1398080, ECO:0000269|PubMed:1404376,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:7651141, ECO:0000269|PubMed:8002589,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:8423144}.
            [CATALYTIC ACTIVITY] Reaction=ATP + H2O = ADP + phosphate + H(+);
            Xref=Rhea:RHEA:13065, ChEBI:CHEBI:15377, ChEBI:CHEBI:15378,
            ChEBI:CHEBI:30616, ChEBI:CHEBI:43474, ChEBI:CHEBI:456216;
            Evidence={ECO:0000250|UniProtKB:Q8YUT0}.
            [SUBUNIT] Forms homodimers, forms a GvpN1-GvpO1 heterodimer,
            interacts with GvpC1 (via the latter's C-terminus) and GvpL, might
            interact with GvpA1. {ECO:0000269|PubMed:35966690}.
            [SUBCELLULAR LOCATION] Gas vesicle {ECO:0000269|PubMed:21158390}.
            Cytoplasm {ECO:0000305|PubMed:21158390}. Note=Probably on the
            exterior surface of the gas vesicle. {ECO:0000305|PubMed:1404376}.
            [INDUCTION] Maximally transcribed in late log phase, probably part
            of a gvpC1-gvpN1 operon (Probable) (PubMed:1956294). Gas vesicles
            appear earlier when grown in static culture, possibly due to
            O(2)-limitation (PubMed:33711860). {ECO:0000269|PubMed:1956294,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:33711860, ECO:0000305|PubMed:7651141,
            ECO:0000305|PubMed:8423144}.
            [DISRUPTION PHENOTYPE] Many small variable-shaped gas vesicles, in
            situ (PubMed:8002589). Makes very few gas vesicles, in H.volcanii
            (PubMed:10894744, PubMed:8606186). {ECO:0000269|PubMed:10894744,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:8002589, ECO:0000269|PubMed:8606186}.
            [MISCELLANEOUS] Encoded in a 14-gene plasmid locus called p-vac
            which produces predominantly short, spindle-shaped gas vesicles
            during all stages of growth. {ECO:0000269|PubMed:1404376,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:1956294, ECO:0000269|PubMed:33711860}.
            [SIMILARITY] Belongs to the CbbQ/NirQ/NorQ/GpvN family.
            {ECO:0000305}.
